  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 258 PM EDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nHigh pressure situated over southwestern Indiana continues to \nprovide quiet weather. Increasing cumulus driven by boundary layer \nprocesses have expanded in coverage across most of the region. \nAloft, cirrus advances from the west ahead of a shortwave trough \nlocated over the northern Plains.\n\nWinds are light and variable owing to the surface high. Winds are \nexpected to become southerly on Wednesday as the high exits \neastward. Fog is possible again tonight, with cloud cover being the \nmitigating factor compared to previous nights. Cumulus will \ndissipate after sunset, but cirrus will continue to expand aloft. \nThese may remain thin enough to allow for fog development. We will \ncap the mention of fog to patchy for now, given the uncertainty with \ncirrus thickness overnight.\n\nWednesday\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave arrives on Wednesday with the risk for \nscattered showers and thunderstorms. Guidance does not suggest a \nsurface low will develop in association with the shortwave, but \nthere are signs of a weak cold front or boundary drifting southward \nin tandem with it. This boundary could act as a focus for convective \ndevelopment, though convection is possible at any possible at any \npoint during the day Wednesday.\n\nThere are two potential rounds of convection that guidance is \nbeginning to hone in on. The first, early in the day associated with \na weakening upstream MCS from the night before. The second, \nredevelopment during the afternoon along the slow-moving cold front.\n\nModel soundings show weak to modest instability along with poor \nlapse rates. However, fast upper-level flow leading to around 40-\n50kt of shear should allow for some storm-scale organization. Storms \nmay briefly show supercell characteristics before growing into \nclusters, due to the shear vector being largely parallel to the \nfront. Though the potential is low due to poor lapse rates and \ninstability, some sporadic damaging wind gusts cannot be ruled out.\n\nThe primary threat may end up being flash flooding, since the ground \nacross the region is very saturated from recent rains. Guidance \nshows high PWATS around or over 2 inches, along with a deep warm \ncloud layer to promote efficient rainfall production. Storm clusters \nmay organize into bands that train over the same areas, allowing for \nconcentrated pockets of locally very heavy rainfall (1 to 3 inches).\n\nSome uncertainty remains on convective coverage, which may range \nfrom isolated to widespread. This depends on how potent the parent \nshortwave is, with a more compact/intense system allowing for more \nrobust lift and therefore higher convective coverage and vice versa.\n\nThursday through early next week...\n\nThe weak frontal boundary meandering southward over Indiana on \nThursday will keep low precipitation chances in the forecast. \nFurther north, surface high pressure begins to build in providing \nmostly quiet weather conditions. Look for the surface high to build \nfurther south Thursday night into Friday resulting in quiet weather \nconditions. Fog is possible during the overnight period both \nThursday and Friday. Another embedded disturbance moves in on \nSaturday supporting at least low rain chances with sufficient \nmoisture advecting into central Indiana. Long range guidance \ngenerally depicts surface high pressure building in once again late \nweekend into early next week. Large scale subsidence supports quiet \nweather conditions.",
